  "id": "COMPOUND:17374 - PROTEIN:5158",
  "verbalization": "Mollicellin N is implicated in the pathogenesis of EVSA-T through multiple, interconnected mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Mollicellin N acts as a target for several molecular entities, including P2X purinoceptors, which are known to mediate cellular signaling and are involved in various physiological and pathological processes. These purinoceptors are targeted by [(1R)-1-[(5Z)-4-Bromo-5-(Bromomethylidene)-2-Oxofuran-3-Yl]Butyl] Acetate, a compound that has been shown to interact with EVSA-T. This indicates that the interaction between Mollicellin N and EVSA-T may involve a signaling cascade initiated by the activation of P2X receptors, which in turn may modulate the activity of [(1R)-1-[(5Z)-4-Bromo-5-(Bromomethylidene)-2-Oxofuran-3-Yl]Butyl] Acetate.\n\nThe evidence also highlights a potential link between Mollicellin N and EVSA-T through the P2X purinoceptor pathway, suggesting that the compound may influence cellular processes by modulating receptor activity. This mechanism is supported by the presence of multiple pathways converging on EVSA-T, indicating a possible role in downstream signaling events. The integration of these pathways underscores the complexity of the relationship between Mollicellin N and EVSA-T, emphasizing the need for further investigation into the specific molecular interactions and their functional implications.",
